1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
Stacks of rusting automobiles,

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:07,000
mountains of man-made debris,

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:11,640
where tons of refuse come to be torched, wrecked,

4
00:00:11,740 --> 00:00:14,440
and shredded.

5
00:00:14,540 --> 00:00:16,640
But what seems like the end of the road

6
00:00:16,740 --> 00:00:20,260
is really a new lease on life.

7
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:23,260
Now, Junkyards on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:47,543 --> 00:00:50,860
Junkyards, scrapyards, bone yards, call them what you will.

9
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:55,300
They conjure nightmarish images of snarling dogs,

10
00:00:55,400 --> 00:01:00,000
and they're always located on the wrong side of the tracks.

11
00:01:00,001 --> 00:01:02,000
But the name is misleading.

12
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:05,800
They are homes to almost everything that is aged, smashed,

13
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:08,940
or just doesn't work anymore.

14
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:13,180
But unlike garbage, everything in the junkyard has value,

15
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:17,660
either as secondhand goods or as recyclable material.

16
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:20,260
We could call this a resource yard.

17
00:01:20,360 --> 00:01:23,620
We could call this an environmental yard,

18
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:27,400
different from anything you'll ever see anyplace else,

19
00:01:27,401 --> 00:01:32,400
and something that is a key component to American society

20
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:34,200
and the American economy.

21
00:01:34,300 --> 00:01:36,000
Go to a junkyard.

22
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:41,840
Just take a slow look around, because you're not looking at junk.

23
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:44,860
You're looking at money.

24
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:47,560
Money by the ton.

25
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:50,360
Just as currency comes in all shapes and sizes,

26
00:01:50,460 --> 00:01:53,060
so do junkyards and the material in them.

27
00:01:53,061 --> 00:01:58,060
The metal scrapyard is just one type of junkyard.

28
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:04,060
Anything made of metal, from industrial equipment

29
00:02:04,061 --> 00:02:08,060
to household appliances, is accepted.

30
00:02:08,061 --> 00:02:11,060
Eventually, everything here will be sold to metal manufacturers

31
00:02:11,061 --> 00:02:14,060
for recycling.

32
00:02:14,061 --> 00:02:18,060
Steel from demolished buildings, railroad tracks,

33
00:02:18,061 --> 00:02:22,060
and crushed automobiles seem to chaotically litter these yards.

34
00:02:22,061 --> 00:02:23,060
these yards.

35
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:31,700
But everything here has a price, purpose, and place.

36
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:32,800
The trucks are coming in.

37
00:02:32,900 --> 00:02:36,000
Basically, they come in and they dump at different areas

38
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:40,120
in the yard, depending on the commodity of the material.

39
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:42,780
With each truck bringing nearly 80,000 pounds

40
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:47,880
of metal, the scrapyard can run out of space fast.

41
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:50,860
For workers, compressing and compacting the huge piles of

42
00:02:50,861 --> 00:02:54,860
part of the job. The mill is the tool of choice

43
00:02:54,861 --> 00:03:01,860
for light gauge metal. The adage separation is painful

44
00:03:01,861 --> 00:03:08,860
has never been truer. The mill at the Hugo New

45
00:03:08,861 --> 00:03:12,860
Proler Scrapyard in Long Beach, California has been operating for

46
00:03:12,861 --> 00:03:17,860
nearly 40 years. The mill is a tool of choice

47
00:03:17,861 --> 00:03:22,460
Old cars make up a majority of its diet.

48
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:26,800
Multiple conveyor belts move the metal into and out

49
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:29,300
of the machine.

50
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:34,740
Inside this tower lies the heart of the contraption, the grinder.

51
00:03:34,840 --> 00:03:39,320
An enormous drum, bristling with 24 swivel hammers,

52
00:03:39,420 --> 00:03:44,920
spins at almost 450 revolutions per minute,

53
00:03:44,921 --> 00:03:47,920
pulverizing everything it touches.

54
00:03:53,920 --> 00:03:57,760
Grinding takes its toll on the mill, and the hammers must be

55
00:03:57,761 --> 00:04:00,260
periodically replaced.

56
00:04:00,261 --> 00:04:03,180
Workers remove the housing and move into the belly of the

57
00:04:03,181 --> 00:04:04,680
beast.

58
00:04:04,681 --> 00:04:08,280
With safety locks in place, the men carefully detach the old

59
00:04:08,281 --> 00:04:09,680
hammers.

60
00:04:09,681 --> 00:04:13,880
The new hammers, weighing nearly 400 pounds,

61
00:04:13,881 --> 00:04:17,580
400 pounds each are lowered in by crane and secured.

62
00:04:20,580 --> 00:04:23,580
With the mill back in action, pounded debris streams

63
00:04:23,620 --> 00:04:25,620
from the grinding chamber.

64
00:04:25,660 --> 00:04:29,320
It goes across a sequence of magnets and through a blower,

65
00:04:29,360 --> 00:04:33,540
which sucks off all of the non-metallics, along with the

66
00:04:33,560 --> 00:04:39,940
carpet, the star form, and that goes into another direction.

67
00:04:39,980 --> 00:04:42,040
A conveyor belt deposits the scrap

68
00:04:42,041 --> 00:04:45,340
the scrap back into the yard.

69
00:04:45,440 --> 00:04:48,980
Because of their mill, Hugo Nuproler boasts one of the largest

70
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:51,580
parking lots in the world.

71
00:04:51,680 --> 00:04:55,180
Only to find your car, you'll need to bring a shovel.

72
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:02,200
But some of the metal that enters the scrapyard

73
00:05:02,300 --> 00:05:05,300
is even too tough for the mill.

74
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:07,200
Any material over a quarter inch thick

75
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:09,640
is sent to the 2,000 ton shearer.

76
00:05:12,040 --> 00:05:19,040
Up to six tons of scrap is loaded into the shearer by a grappling claw crane.

77
00:05:31,040 --> 00:05:36,040
Hydraulic compactors bend and fold the metal until it's shaped like a loaf of bread.

78
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:45,280
A 32-foot-long gather cylinder pushes the scrap

79
00:05:45,380 --> 00:05:46,280
towards the blades.

80
00:05:53,380 --> 00:05:56,180
The giant shears chew through the emerging scrap.

81
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:02,400
The cutting surface is actually five blades, three above and two

82
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:04,500
on the bottom.

83
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:10,400
Each is 34 inches long and nearly five inches thick.

84
00:06:10,401 --> 00:06:12,800
The Scrapp Yard is a processing plant,

85
00:06:12,900 --> 00:06:15,280
and the metal doesn't stay long.

86
00:06:15,380 --> 00:06:17,940
Most of the material leaves the Hugo Nuproler Yard

87
00:06:18,040 --> 00:06:19,940
on ocean-going scrap ships.

88
00:06:22,140 --> 00:06:24,480
This ship, bound for a Korean steel mill,

89
00:06:24,580 --> 00:06:30,360
is over 600 feet in length and loaded with nearly 30,000 tons.

90
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:39,220
Most people's junk is another person's goal.

91
00:06:39,221 --> 00:06:41,020
You know, it's a gold mine.

92
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:42,520
We don't look at it as junk.

93
00:06:42,620 --> 00:06:43,860
We look at it as scrap.

94
00:06:43,960 --> 00:06:47,160
And it's worth something to everybody.

95
00:06:47,260 --> 00:06:49,860
And that has always been true.

96
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:54,360
Recycling and reusing things is nothing new.

97
00:06:54,460 --> 00:06:56,880
For early humans, gathering raw materials

98
00:06:56,980 --> 00:06:59,380
was extremely difficult.

99
00:06:59,480 --> 00:07:02,940
Miners in 4500 BC struggled to collect copper

100
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:06,140
in the mountains of what is today Serbia.

101
00:07:06,141 --> 00:07:10,380
They managed to dig multiple shafts, some up to 60 feet deep,

102
00:07:10,480 --> 00:07:13,380
using picks made from deer antlers.

103
00:07:13,480 --> 00:07:16,180
With commodities acquired at such a high price,

104
00:07:16,280 --> 00:07:19,780
early humans were very frugal, finding it easier to reuse

105
00:07:19,880 --> 00:07:22,260
something than to create it anew.

106
00:07:22,360 --> 00:07:27,320
I think honestly that human beings recycled as soon as they

107
00:07:27,420 --> 00:07:29,460
made the first artifact.

108
00:07:29,560 --> 00:07:34,460
They always focused on ways to use something

109
00:07:34,461 --> 00:07:38,700
in as many ways as possible, and then if it broke,

110
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:42,500
to figure out another way they could use it.

111
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:45,600
Early craftsmen took recycling one step further

112
00:07:45,700 --> 00:07:48,100
by reusing pieces from abandoned structures

113
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:50,400
to create new buildings.

114
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:52,540
Pieces from some of the greatest monuments

115
00:07:52,640 --> 00:07:57,320
of the ancient world found their way into new creations.

116
00:07:57,420 --> 00:08:00,780
In the 1500s, Spanish conquistadors in Mexico

117
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:04,460
destroyed Aztec buildings to construct their own cities.

118
00:08:04,461 --> 00:08:06,460
centuries.

119
00:08:06,461 --> 00:08:09,460
Centuries before, parts of the Roman Colosseum

120
00:08:09,461 --> 00:08:12,760
morphed into St. Peter's Basilica.

121
00:08:12,761 --> 00:08:14,640
Even the mighty Colossus of Rhodes

122
00:08:14,641 --> 00:08:18,780
was scrapped by invaders to make tools and weapons.

123
00:08:18,781 --> 00:08:21,700
Part of the reason that metal was so important to these people

124
00:08:21,701 --> 00:08:26,220
was it took a tremendous amount of skill and energy

125
00:08:26,221 --> 00:08:28,620
to forge it into the original implement,

126
00:08:28,621 --> 00:08:31,860
whether it was a helmet or a sword or chain mail

127
00:08:31,861 --> 00:08:34,460
or whatever else.

128
00:08:34,461 --> 00:08:36,300
Weapons such as swords and spears

129
00:08:36,400 --> 00:08:39,960
could ensure a king's authority and were prized by early royalty

130
00:08:40,060 --> 00:08:42,700
and scrap collectors alike.

131
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:45,500
Archeologists that dig up battlefields

132
00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:48,200
are really frustrated people.

133
00:08:48,300 --> 00:08:50,780
Because originally, years ago, we

134
00:08:50,880 --> 00:08:52,880
thought that when you dig up a battlefield,

135
00:08:52,980 --> 00:08:57,020
you're going to find helmets and swords and chain mail

136
00:08:57,120 --> 00:08:58,620
and all this kind of stuff.

137
00:08:58,720 --> 00:09:00,860
Uh-uh.

138
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:03,180
On one such battlefield, the fate of England

139
00:09:03,181 --> 00:09:04,680
decided.

140
00:09:04,780 --> 00:09:08,620
In 1066, 7,000 Norman troops invaded Britain

141
00:09:08,720 --> 00:09:11,420
under the command of William I.

142
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:15,320
The English king, Harold II, raised an army of 7,000

143
00:09:15,420 --> 00:09:17,020
to repulse the attack.

144
00:09:17,120 --> 00:09:20,940
The two forces met on the battlefield of Hastings.

145
00:09:21,040 --> 00:09:25,040
When the fighting had ended, nearly 3,500 men lay dead,

146
00:09:25,140 --> 00:09:28,380
including the English king, clearing the way for William

147
00:09:28,480 --> 00:09:32,480
to become England's first Norman monarch.

148
00:09:32,481 --> 00:09:34,980
In 1966, the British archaeologists

149
00:09:35,080 --> 00:09:37,920
decided that they would dig up the battlefield,

150
00:09:38,020 --> 00:09:39,320
because they had all these reports.

151
00:09:39,420 --> 00:09:40,720
They knew where the battlefield was.

152
00:09:40,820 --> 00:09:43,720
They knew where the archers were, and the longbowmen were,

153
00:09:43,820 --> 00:09:46,720
and the spearmen were, and the sword fighters were,

154
00:09:46,820 --> 00:09:47,920
and all this.

155
00:09:48,020 --> 00:09:49,760
And they were going to recreate the battle

156
00:09:49,860 --> 00:09:51,760
in the archaeological remains.

157
00:09:51,860 --> 00:09:55,440
And all they got were some teeth and bones,

158
00:09:55,540 --> 00:09:57,900
and very little else.

159
00:09:58,000 --> 00:09:59,480
The metal is gone.

160
00:09:59,580 --> 00:10:02,040
And the reason it's gone is the dead soldiers

161
00:10:02,041 --> 00:10:08,280
picked clean for any valuables, especially metal.

162
00:10:08,340 --> 00:10:11,720
The dead weren't the only ones plundered for scrap metal.

163
00:10:11,780 --> 00:10:15,360
When the pirate Captain Kidd was captured in 1699,

164
00:10:15,420 --> 00:10:20,020
included in his treasure was 10 tons of scrap iron.

165
00:10:20,100 --> 00:10:23,300
But the Industrial Revolution and cheap mass-produced steel

166
00:10:23,360 --> 00:10:26,260
removed metal from the most wanted list.

167
00:10:26,340 --> 00:10:28,160
With the Industrial Revolution,

168
00:10:28,240 --> 00:10:29,960
there were a lot of scrap yards.

169
00:10:29,961 --> 00:10:34,960
But the real focus for the scrap dealers was rags.

170
00:10:35,060 --> 00:10:38,700
And the important thing was the value of those rags

171
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:42,900
in making paper to the point where a ton of rags,

172
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
for most of the time from the 1600s all the way up

173
00:10:46,100 --> 00:10:50,520
to the 1900s, was as valuable relatively

174
00:10:50,620 --> 00:10:53,620
as a ton of aluminum is today.

175
00:10:53,720 --> 00:10:55,720
It was really where the money was.

176
00:10:55,721 --> 00:10:58,720
was.

177
00:10:58,820 --> 00:11:01,360
But Western railroad expansion in the 1800s

178
00:11:01,460 --> 00:11:05,060
changed the rag business forever.

179
00:11:05,160 --> 00:11:08,460
With new access to a seemingly endless forest at hand,

180
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:12,480
companies learned to mass produce paper from lumber.

181
00:11:12,580 --> 00:11:16,380
The vagrant rag pickers were out of a job.

182
00:11:16,480 --> 00:11:18,380
The ones that chose to stay in the junk business

183
00:11:18,480 --> 00:11:20,380
were forced to shift their focus.

184
00:11:22,580 --> 00:11:24,880
Metal, wagon parts, and building materials

185
00:11:24,881 --> 00:11:29,120
became the lifeblood for the junk industry.

186
00:11:29,220 --> 00:11:31,680
The goods collected were resold as secondhand,

187
00:11:31,780 --> 00:11:35,320
or broken down to be recycled.

188
00:11:35,420 --> 00:11:38,400
The 19th century image of the junk man and his horse-drawn

189
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:41,960
wagon became an enduring, albeit low-class symbol

190
00:11:42,060 --> 00:11:44,160
of American society.

191
00:11:44,260 --> 00:11:48,100
My mother right now is 100 years old, almost 101.

192
00:11:48,200 --> 00:11:50,900
And she always told the story about the Schein brothers

193
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:52,640
from Spencer.

194
00:11:52,641 --> 00:11:55,780
They came around on the farm every spring,

195
00:11:55,880 --> 00:11:58,980
and they would ask if we had any old pots and pans,

196
00:11:59,080 --> 00:12:01,140
and they would trade it for a broom.

197
00:12:01,240 --> 00:12:03,120
And they came around with a horse and buggy

198
00:12:03,220 --> 00:12:05,760
to pick up this stuff.

199
00:12:05,860 --> 00:12:08,560
But the often maligned junkyard and junk industry

200
00:12:08,660 --> 00:12:11,220
managed to gain popularity during the First and Second

201
00:12:11,320 --> 00:12:14,160
World Wars.

202
00:12:14,260 --> 00:12:18,000
Scrap metal from the yards helped feed the war machine.

203
00:12:18,100 --> 00:12:21,840
With World War II, metal took on an even more important

204
00:12:21,841 --> 00:12:23,580
strategic role.

205
00:12:23,680 --> 00:12:27,040
And the government began to pack rat everything.

206
00:12:27,140 --> 00:12:31,320
And it was scrap dealers, scrap paper, scrap metal,

207
00:12:31,420 --> 00:12:33,260
who were part of the core of this,

208
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:37,520
and who were really seeing to it that our war effort

209
00:12:37,620 --> 00:12:38,860
had a metal backbone.

210
00:12:41,560 --> 00:12:43,360
Despite their wartime triumph,

211
00:12:43,460 --> 00:12:46,800
junkyards rarely received the respect they deserved.

212
00:12:46,900 --> 00:12:49,240
During the 1960s, Lady Bird Johnson

213
00:12:49,241 --> 00:12:52,040
to keep America beautiful.

214
00:12:52,140 --> 00:12:56,040
The humble junkyard was one of her first targets.

215
00:12:56,140 --> 00:12:59,120
Lawmakers passed ordinances prohibiting yards to exist

216
00:12:59,220 --> 00:13:01,480
within eyesight of major highways.

217
00:13:01,580 --> 00:13:04,560
Many junkyards were forced to build large perimeter walls,

218
00:13:04,660 --> 00:13:08,000
not to keep out intruders, but to keep from being seen.

219
00:13:11,060 --> 00:13:14,440
Today, these one-time eyesores are getting a second look.

220
00:13:19,240 --> 00:13:21,980
Evolving thoughts about the economy and ecology

221
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:24,680
have changed the junkyard's image.

222
00:13:24,780 --> 00:13:27,480
And what was once considered secondhand garbage

223
00:13:27,580 --> 00:13:29,480
is now seen as pure gold.

224
00:13:38,700 --> 00:13:39,900
The End of the Road.

225
00:13:45,400 --> 00:13:48,400
Since the first car has rolled off Henry Ford's assembly line,

226
00:13:48,401 --> 00:13:52,400
Americans have loved their automobiles.

227
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:58,400
But when love dies, it ends up here, the automobile salvage

228
00:13:58,500 --> 00:13:59,340
yard.

229
00:13:59,440 --> 00:14:03,440
In the US, nearly 11 million cars are retired each year,

230
00:14:03,540 --> 00:14:06,460
enough to fill a four-lane highway from New York to Los

231
00:14:06,560 --> 00:14:08,960
Angeles.

232
00:14:09,060 --> 00:14:11,960
Yet it's rare to see even one vehicle abandoned on the side

233
00:14:12,060 --> 00:14:13,360
of the road.

234
00:14:13,460 --> 00:14:16,860
That's because every car has life left in it, if you know where

235
00:14:16,861 --> 00:14:18,900
if you know where to look.

236
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:23,760
You would really be surprised at the level of expertise

237
00:14:23,860 --> 00:14:26,500
in a place like this, in a junkyard.

238
00:14:26,600 --> 00:14:32,200
These are people that can divide hubcaps into 150 or 200 types,

239
00:14:32,300 --> 00:14:35,780
know exactly where they are, know exactly how to clean them up,

240
00:14:35,880 --> 00:14:40,320
get them out, put them on your car, and get you out of there.

241
00:14:40,420 --> 00:14:43,560
By reselling used parts, automobile salvage yards

242
00:14:43,660 --> 00:14:46,420
have perfected ways of squeezing every last mile

243
00:14:46,421 --> 00:14:51,720
out of an automobile. They are modern-day assembly lines,

244
00:14:51,820 --> 00:14:57,560
in reverse. In a traditional auto wrecking yard,

245
00:14:57,660 --> 00:15:02,100
workers dismantle and meticulously catalog every piece.

246
00:15:02,200 --> 00:15:08,500
Body panels are stacked, engines pulled, and electronics are

247
00:15:08,600 --> 00:15:14,740
gutted from the wrecked heap. The goal is to remove everything

248
00:15:14,741 --> 00:15:16,245
that can be resold.

249
00:15:16,246 --> 00:15:22,740
And this has been going on for nearly as long as there have been cars on the road.

250
00:15:22,741 --> 00:15:30,740
A gentleman named Johnny Carter down in Houston claims to be the first automotive dismantler in the United States.

251
00:15:30,741 --> 00:15:35,117
They started somewhere in the 1910, 1915 era, as I recall.

252
00:15:35,118 --> 00:15:38,740
Generally, it started on the edge of every city,

253
00:15:38,741 --> 00:15:41,740
city on the cheaper ground where people would get ahold of

254
00:15:41,840 --> 00:15:45,940
abandoned cars and start selling some parts off of them.

255
00:15:46,040 --> 00:15:48,880
Early entrepreneurs had to devise creative ways

256
00:15:48,980 --> 00:15:51,480
to separate the car from its worthwhile parts.

257
00:15:51,580 --> 00:15:55,900
When we first got into the automobile salvage business,

258
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:58,200
the automobiles, as they came in,

259
00:15:58,300 --> 00:16:00,100
we wouldn't get a little gas out of the tank,

260
00:16:00,200 --> 00:16:01,900
put a match in, we'd burn them up.

261
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:05,640
We burned them because this got rid of all of the perishable

262
00:16:05,740 --> 00:16:08,740
things, the upholstery, the wood, because we had to get

263
00:16:08,741 --> 00:16:13,080
Most of the cars were half wood, and the other half was steel.

264
00:16:15,840 --> 00:16:18,780
Early tools of the trade were only slightly more advanced

265
00:16:18,781 --> 00:16:21,180
than fire.

266
00:16:21,181 --> 00:16:24,180
We got our first cutting torch in about 1940.

267
00:16:24,181 --> 00:16:26,460
I was just a kid, but I remember when they came,

268
00:16:26,461 --> 00:16:30,160
they were demonstrating it, how to use it.

269
00:16:30,161 --> 00:16:33,700
Prior to that, we used cold cut and hammer and sledge.

270
00:16:33,701 --> 00:16:37,060
I think that's where the name auto wrecker probably came into

271
00:16:37,061 --> 00:16:39,960
because we were wrecking them with chisels and hammers.

272
00:16:40,060 --> 00:16:42,300
Then the first cutting torch came in.

273
00:16:42,400 --> 00:16:45,800
Then we could start cutting the cars up into pieces.

274
00:16:45,900 --> 00:16:47,800
One tool used since the early days

275
00:16:47,900 --> 00:16:51,500
was the car crusher, but only big automobile manufacturers

276
00:16:51,600 --> 00:16:54,420
could afford one.

277
00:16:54,520 --> 00:16:57,620
In the early 1970s, mechanically inclined yard workers

278
00:16:57,720 --> 00:17:00,920
began to build hydraulic crushers, which allowed the cars

279
00:17:01,020 --> 00:17:02,820
to be flattened for storage and transport.

280
00:17:02,821 --> 00:17:07,820
transport.

281
00:17:07,920 --> 00:17:11,360
The owners were always looking for ways to improve business.

282
00:17:11,460 --> 00:17:15,640
An early obstacle was matching parts with prospective buyers.

283
00:17:15,740 --> 00:17:18,600
Resourceful auto wreckers realized by working together,

284
00:17:18,700 --> 00:17:21,000
they could sell more.

285
00:17:21,100 --> 00:17:23,600
Speaker telephones, affectionately known as hoop

286
00:17:23,700 --> 00:17:25,980
holler and shout lines, connected many yards

287
00:17:26,080 --> 00:17:27,640
simultaneously.

288
00:17:27,740 --> 00:17:30,520
It was like the old telephone, except you had a speaker.

289
00:17:30,521 --> 00:17:34,360
And you would ask for a particular part and say,

290
00:17:34,460 --> 00:17:38,620
Spencer needs a transmission for a 55 Chevrolet.

291
00:17:38,720 --> 00:17:42,660
There might be 25 to 50, sometimes even up to 100 yards

292
00:17:42,760 --> 00:17:43,760
on this line.

293
00:17:43,860 --> 00:17:46,460
And if anyone had it, they would come back and say,

294
00:17:46,560 --> 00:17:49,180
I've got the transmission for $50.

295
00:17:49,280 --> 00:17:50,680
And everybody heard it.

296
00:17:50,780 --> 00:17:53,780
And some other man would come in and say, I got one for 45.

297
00:17:53,880 --> 00:17:58,480
The other guy said, I'll say one for 45 laid in.

298
00:17:58,580 --> 00:18:00,280
For many early yard owners, keeping

299
00:18:00,281 --> 00:18:03,620
was an exercise in brute memorization.

300
00:18:03,720 --> 00:18:05,680
Being able to find items in the yard quickly

301
00:18:05,780 --> 00:18:09,920
could be the difference between making or losing a sale.

302
00:18:10,020 --> 00:18:12,520
Forward-thinking wrecking yards were eager to implement

303
00:18:12,620 --> 00:18:15,220
new technology.

304
00:18:15,320 --> 00:18:18,740
In approximately 1980, we got our first computer.

305
00:18:18,840 --> 00:18:22,940
Our first computer was the size of a refrigerator.

306
00:18:23,040 --> 00:18:26,780
Today, the entire industry relies on computers.

307
00:18:26,880 --> 00:18:30,260
Thousands of yards communicate and share inventory via the

308
00:18:30,280 --> 00:18:31,780
the Internet.

309
00:18:31,880 --> 00:18:35,420
Parts are shipped fluidly yard to yard, across the country,

310
00:18:35,520 --> 00:18:37,980
nation to nation, all at the click of a mouse.

311
00:18:41,260 --> 00:18:44,100
Here's the newest wrinkle in auto salvage,

312
00:18:44,200 --> 00:18:46,100
the pick-your-part yard.

313
00:18:46,200 --> 00:18:48,460
Cars are placed on stands, and the public

314
00:18:48,560 --> 00:18:52,060
is allowed to find and remove their own used parts.

315
00:18:52,160 --> 00:18:54,440
Tools in hand, the home mechanic is now

316
00:18:54,540 --> 00:18:56,500
the auto wrecking expert.

317
00:18:56,600 --> 00:18:58,500
This yard is divided into separate sections

318
00:18:58,501 --> 00:19:01,460
by product line, the Ford section, the truck section,

319
00:19:01,560 --> 00:19:04,240
General Motors section, Chevy section, and imports.

320
00:19:04,340 --> 00:19:06,240
And the customers can come out and they go to a particular

321
00:19:06,340 --> 00:19:10,140
section if that's the type of vehicle that they're looking for.

322
00:19:10,240 --> 00:19:12,640
But before the cars reach the public lot,

323
00:19:12,740 --> 00:19:15,040
they're processed for both personal and environmental

324
00:19:15,140 --> 00:19:17,060
safety.

325
00:19:17,160 --> 00:19:18,560
Beginning at the arrival of the vehicle,

326
00:19:18,660 --> 00:19:21,260
when it comes to pick your part, it arrives at our drop off

327
00:19:21,360 --> 00:19:23,360
area, removing the fluids, the license plates,

328
00:19:23,460 --> 00:19:24,760
and any trash from the car.

329
00:19:24,860 --> 00:19:26,660
Then it goes to the oil rack, and they

330
00:19:26,661 --> 00:19:29,560
the existing fluids that aren't underneath the hood, for instance,

331
00:19:29,660 --> 00:19:31,160
that are accessible underneath the car.

332
00:19:31,260 --> 00:19:33,100
They remove the engine oil, transmission oil,

333
00:19:33,200 --> 00:19:35,600
rear end grease.

334
00:19:35,700 --> 00:19:40,400
Forklifts suspend the cars on metal racks.

335
00:19:40,500 --> 00:19:42,640
A special drill punctures the car's gas tank

336
00:19:42,740 --> 00:19:44,220
and siphons any remaining fuel.

337
00:19:47,120 --> 00:19:51,320
The fluids are collected in hoppers below the racks.

338
00:19:51,420 --> 00:19:53,960
Workers carefully remove the hazardous engine coolant

339
00:19:53,961 --> 00:19:59,800
gas for proper disposal, while the oil, like almost everything

340
00:19:59,900 --> 00:20:04,340
else in the junkyard, will be saved for recycling.

341
00:20:04,440 --> 00:20:06,040
The process is quick.

342
00:20:06,140 --> 00:20:08,200
In as little as an hour, the car can

343
00:20:08,300 --> 00:20:11,540
join the nearly 2,300 other vehicles on display.

344
00:20:14,380 --> 00:20:18,520
Pick Your Part yards have become phenomenally successful.

345
00:20:18,620 --> 00:20:22,220
At this California yard, between 60 and 200 cars

346
00:20:22,221 --> 00:20:25,460
200 cars arrive every day.

347
00:20:25,560 --> 00:20:28,460
And on a busy Saturday, as many as 4,000 customers

348
00:20:28,560 --> 00:20:32,660
can be found pulling off fenders or prowling under the hood.

349
00:20:32,760 --> 00:20:34,140
There was a substantial amount of thought

350
00:20:34,240 --> 00:20:36,400
put into configuration of the yard here.

351
00:20:36,500 --> 00:20:39,300
Cars are aligned hood to hood and trunk to trunk

352
00:20:39,400 --> 00:20:41,940
to allow for an aisle way for the engine hoist to go through

353
00:20:42,040 --> 00:20:44,240
to make it easier for the customer to remove the engines.

354
00:20:44,340 --> 00:20:47,980
It also maximizes our space.

355
00:20:48,080 --> 00:20:49,940
The yard invests heavily in machines

356
00:20:49,941 --> 00:20:53,080
the sprawling scrap under control.

357
00:20:53,180 --> 00:20:54,580
This is one gas tank.

358
00:20:54,680 --> 00:20:57,080
This is the condensed version of about 30 gas tanks.

359
00:21:07,800 --> 00:21:10,500
Condensing these gas tanks from a storage perspective

360
00:21:10,600 --> 00:21:13,200
works out to be a lot better for us and more efficient

361
00:21:13,300 --> 00:21:15,900
from a space perspective so that we can have a little more room

362
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:17,800
to run other aspects of our operation.

363
00:21:19,940 --> 00:21:24,940
Even the cars on display aren't immune.

364
00:21:25,040 --> 00:21:28,440
The vehicles will stay in the yard for up to two months.

365
00:21:28,540 --> 00:21:31,480
When the time is up, the car is sold to a scrapyard

366
00:21:31,580 --> 00:21:34,380
regardless of how many parts have been removed.

367
00:21:34,480 --> 00:21:36,700
This policy keeps the inventory fresh,

368
00:21:36,800 --> 00:21:39,000
but it also means that the outgoing vehicles

369
00:21:39,100 --> 00:21:42,200
must be processed as quickly as the incoming.

370
00:21:42,300 --> 00:21:45,040
Some of the car parts are made from aluminum,

371
00:21:45,140 --> 00:21:47,700
which is more expensive than the car parts.

372
00:21:47,701 --> 00:21:50,040
are made from aluminum, which is more expensive

373
00:21:50,140 --> 00:21:54,100
and will fetch a higher price than the steel frame and body.

374
00:21:54,200 --> 00:21:56,700
The salvage yard dismantles or wrecks the car

375
00:21:56,800 --> 00:21:59,840
to separate the different types of metal.

376
00:21:59,940 --> 00:22:01,640
The first thing to go is the engine.

377
00:22:04,780 --> 00:22:07,180
And the pick your part yard has a special machine

378
00:22:07,280 --> 00:22:10,020
just for that purpose.

379
00:22:10,120 --> 00:22:13,120
This particular machine is one of two in existence.

380
00:22:13,220 --> 00:22:15,400
If an individual was to pull an engine out of the car,

381
00:22:15,500 --> 00:22:17,700
it might take him up to two hours to do it.

382
00:22:17,701 --> 00:22:20,940
This machine, however, take a matter of just a few seconds

383
00:22:21,040 --> 00:22:23,200
to yank an engine off of its engine mount.

384
00:22:26,180 --> 00:22:27,340
Just put it aside.

385
00:22:33,340 --> 00:22:37,980
Once the engine is removed, the giant claw effortlessly

386
00:22:38,080 --> 00:22:40,060
yanks any remaining valuable metal.

387
00:22:42,160 --> 00:22:45,220
The heater core and the AC condenser,

388
00:22:45,221 --> 00:22:47,620
also the radiator pulled, and the aluminum bumpers,

389
00:22:47,720 --> 00:22:49,800
because that has more value beyond just the scrap value

390
00:22:49,900 --> 00:22:50,900
of the flat car.

391
00:22:52,760 --> 00:22:54,900
The gutted vehicle is unceremoniously

392
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:56,700
dropped into the compactor.

393
00:23:01,340 --> 00:23:04,400
It's crushed between 12 and 14 inches high,

394
00:23:04,500 --> 00:23:07,000
as the roof slowly lowers on its helpless victim.

395
00:23:09,180 --> 00:23:11,720
When the process is finished, another car is added.

396
00:23:11,721 --> 00:23:15,720
up to four will be smashed into one block of metal.

397
00:23:15,721 --> 00:23:18,720
The crushed car bodies are loaded onto flatbed trucks

398
00:23:18,721 --> 00:23:20,720
for shipping.

399
00:23:20,721 --> 00:23:24,720
The driver carefully inspects the load for loose pieces that

400
00:23:24,721 --> 00:23:27,720
may blow off during transport.

401
00:23:27,721 --> 00:23:31,720
For these cars, this truly is the end of the road.

402
00:23:31,721 --> 00:23:35,720
Their final destination is a scrapyard mill, where they'll

403
00:23:35,721 --> 00:23:38,720
be ground into pieces small enough to fit in your hand.

404
00:23:41,720 --> 00:23:44,720
But from there, the metal may turn up in any number

405
00:23:44,820 --> 00:23:47,520
of new products, even new automobiles,

406
00:23:47,620 --> 00:23:50,720
which means one of these cars, in some small way,

407
00:23:50,820 --> 00:23:52,660
may find itself back on the road.

408
00:24:00,280 --> 00:24:03,680
Yes, personal computers have changed the world.

409
00:24:03,780 --> 00:24:06,080
But as we all know too well, the constant increase

410
00:24:06,180 --> 00:24:09,320
in speed and computing power makes machines obsolete almost

411
00:24:09,420 --> 00:24:11,720
as soon as they are built.

412
00:24:11,721 --> 00:24:14,520
The electronic junkyard is the byproduct of a technology

413
00:24:14,521 --> 00:24:16,820
evolving at light speed.

414
00:24:16,821 --> 00:24:20,960
You can recycle anything as long as you have enough of it.

415
00:24:20,961 --> 00:24:25,260
There are a lot of companies that are recycling computers.

416
00:24:25,261 --> 00:24:28,140
They still haven't gotten to a threshold level

417
00:24:28,141 --> 00:24:31,400
where everybody can say, well, when I finish with my computer,

418
00:24:31,401 --> 00:24:34,540
I'm going to take it over there and sell it to them.

419
00:24:34,541 --> 00:24:36,640
But that's quickly changing.

420
00:24:36,641 --> 00:24:38,820
Hundreds of thousands of outdated computers

421
00:24:38,821 --> 00:24:40,840
and other electronic equipment end up

422
00:24:40,841 --> 00:24:42,880
like this.

423
00:24:42,980 --> 00:24:45,180
United Recycling Industries in Chicago

424
00:24:45,280 --> 00:24:47,580
understands the machines may be obsolete,

425
00:24:47,680 --> 00:24:49,880
but they still have something to offer.

426
00:24:49,980 --> 00:24:53,480
The high-tech junkyard is a relatively new phenomenon.

427
00:24:53,580 --> 00:24:57,360
In the 1950s and 60s, few computers existed,

428
00:24:57,460 --> 00:24:59,320
but the ones that did were a treasure trove

429
00:24:59,420 --> 00:25:01,600
of precious metals.

430
00:25:01,700 --> 00:25:05,160
Gold connectors, silver wire insets, and copper circuit

431
00:25:05,260 --> 00:25:09,660
boards were the brains of these electronic thinking machines.

432
00:25:09,661 --> 00:25:15,360
You had a mainframe computer that would take up 15,000 square feet.

433
00:25:15,680 --> 00:25:21,560
By the time you throw in the CPUs, the generators, the coolers, and everything else,

434
00:25:21,740 --> 00:25:26,120
you're talking 20,000 square feet for something that had like 20 meg of memory.

435
00:25:26,600 --> 00:25:33,100
Back in the 60s and 70s, a ton of printed circuit boards probably had 30 to 40 ounces of gold.

436
00:25:33,900 --> 00:25:37,120
A good circuit board today is probably 8 to 9 ounces per ton of material.

437
00:25:37,121 --> 00:25:39,520
So while there is a lot of gold in this material,

438
00:25:39,620 --> 00:25:43,360
it's only there because you have so much volume.

439
00:25:43,460 --> 00:25:46,120
That volume is due in part to the personal computer

440
00:25:46,220 --> 00:25:49,260
revolution of the early 1980s.

441
00:25:49,360 --> 00:25:52,240
Driving this explosive market was the tiny integrated

442
00:25:52,340 --> 00:25:53,500
circuit.

443
00:25:53,600 --> 00:25:57,440
These chips were the power behind the marvelous machines.

444
00:25:57,540 --> 00:25:59,440
Unfortunately, many early scrappers

445
00:25:59,540 --> 00:26:01,780
continued to focus on the precious metals,

446
00:26:01,880 --> 00:26:07,120
never realizing the real money was in reselling the chips.

447
00:26:07,121 --> 00:26:10,120
Some brand new chips are worth hundreds of dollars,

448
00:26:10,220 --> 00:26:12,520
and the used ones can work just as well.

449
00:26:12,620 --> 00:26:15,820
You could have a board that had 25, 50 cents worth of gold

450
00:26:15,920 --> 00:26:18,560
and $2,000 worth of integrated circuits.

451
00:26:18,660 --> 00:26:19,860
It was mind boggling.

452
00:26:19,960 --> 00:26:22,060
And we were just merely going along our way trying to get,

453
00:26:22,160 --> 00:26:25,880
you know, your $300, $500 gold price at 9 ounces,

454
00:26:25,980 --> 00:26:30,580
10 ounces per ton, and throwing away $20,000 per ton in circuits.

455
00:26:30,680 --> 00:26:32,780
So in 1990, we got smart real fast.

456
00:26:32,781 --> 00:26:35,780
fast.

457
00:26:35,880 --> 00:26:39,520
Today, electronic junkyards are more savvy.

458
00:26:39,620 --> 00:26:41,480
The machines that enter these recycling plants

459
00:26:41,580 --> 00:26:43,120
are picked clean.

460
00:26:43,220 --> 00:26:46,420
Nothing is taken for granted, and nothing is wasted.

461
00:26:46,520 --> 00:26:49,400
The circuit boards behind you were all manually extracted

462
00:26:49,500 --> 00:26:51,900
out of electronics equipment.

463
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,660
In this phase here is where we would remove the individual

464
00:26:54,760 --> 00:26:56,200
circuits themselves.

465
00:26:56,300 --> 00:26:58,280
These are almost like black gold.

466
00:26:58,380 --> 00:27:01,240
After this step, the printed circuit board is then sent on

467
00:27:01,241 --> 00:27:04,540
for precious metal recovery, gold, silver, platinum,

468
00:27:04,640 --> 00:27:06,640
and palladium.

469
00:27:06,740 --> 00:27:08,540
Once everything of value is removed,

470
00:27:08,640 --> 00:27:11,680
the machines are sent to the crusher.

471
00:27:11,780 --> 00:27:14,420
Scrap equipment is loaded into the bin tipper, which is then

472
00:27:14,520 --> 00:27:17,160
dumped out onto the conveyor belt.

473
00:27:17,260 --> 00:27:19,220
That is loaded into a primary shredder, which

474
00:27:19,320 --> 00:27:22,360
is a 750 horsepower shear shredder.

475
00:27:22,460 --> 00:27:24,960
Material then is reduced down into four-inch strips.

476
00:27:27,960 --> 00:27:29,940
A series of grinders further reduce

477
00:27:29,941 --> 00:27:32,540
the printers, copiers, and computers into pieces

478
00:27:32,640 --> 00:27:33,640
the size of a quarter.

479
00:27:37,540 --> 00:27:40,980
Shakers and sorters separate the material.

480
00:27:41,080 --> 00:27:43,660
The final stream of debris is sent across a device

481
00:27:43,760 --> 00:27:47,020
called an eddy current.

482
00:27:47,120 --> 00:27:49,760
The eddy current, a kind of magnet in reverse,

483
00:27:49,860 --> 00:27:51,760
charges the metal pieces and throws them

484
00:27:51,860 --> 00:27:54,100
from the conveyor belt.

485
00:27:54,200 --> 00:27:55,940
The metal leaps into a special bin

486
00:27:56,040 --> 00:27:59,540
while the remaining plastic rolls off.

487
00:27:59,541 --> 00:28:02,480
What was once thousands of dollars of digital might

488
00:28:02,580 --> 00:28:05,280
is transformed into pennies worth of plastic, glass,

489
00:28:05,380 --> 00:28:06,080
and copper.

490
00:28:09,580 --> 00:28:11,780
All those pennies do add up.

491
00:28:11,880 --> 00:28:14,580
But the financial gain is only a small part of the electronic

492
00:28:14,680 --> 00:28:15,480
recycling equation.

493
00:28:18,300 --> 00:28:20,800
That's because relatively few computers are recycled.

494
00:28:20,900 --> 00:28:23,500
And the National Safety Council estimates that half a billion

495
00:28:23,600 --> 00:28:29,100
will be packed into American landfills between 1997 and 2007.

496
00:28:29,101 --> 00:28:32,000
Everybody in the government knows that the environmental problems

497
00:28:32,100 --> 00:28:34,040
from electronic scrap is a nightmare.

498
00:28:34,140 --> 00:28:37,240
Even the White House has somebody who is on electronic recycling

499
00:28:37,340 --> 00:28:38,840
on a committee for the president.

500
00:28:38,940 --> 00:28:40,540
Everybody knows this is a nightmare.

501
00:28:40,640 --> 00:28:44,740
If it hasn't already happened, it's right around the corner.

502
00:28:44,840 --> 00:28:46,460
And it's not the first time Americans

503
00:28:46,560 --> 00:28:48,660
have faced this kind of nightmare.

504
00:28:48,760 --> 00:28:50,460
Long before there was high-tech junk,

505
00:28:50,560 --> 00:28:53,660
there was low-tech trash, and it was packing landfills

506
00:28:53,760 --> 00:28:55,500
at an alarming rate.

507
00:28:55,600 --> 00:28:58,700
The problem led to recycling plants that specialize

508
00:28:58,701 --> 00:29:01,500
in rescuing reusable household materials

509
00:29:01,600 --> 00:29:04,100
before they reach the dump.

510
00:29:04,200 --> 00:29:06,800
Most people in our country regard recycling

511
00:29:06,900 --> 00:29:09,580
as their disposal, but actually, it's a large industry

512
00:29:09,680 --> 00:29:11,480
in the United States. The industry today

513
00:29:11,580 --> 00:29:14,780
is in excess of $20 billion, which is approximately half

514
00:29:14,880 --> 00:29:16,480
the size of the US steel business.

515
00:29:19,880 --> 00:29:23,800
In 1970, the first Earth Day was held to draw attention

516
00:29:23,900 --> 00:29:27,460
to environmental problems.

517
00:29:27,461 --> 00:29:29,860
One of many concerns was overcrowded landfills.

518
00:29:33,060 --> 00:29:35,940
Recycling household products such as cans, bottles,

519
00:29:36,040 --> 00:29:39,780
and plastic containers seemed a simple solution.

520
00:29:39,880 --> 00:29:42,640
So high-tech recycling plants were built.

521
00:29:42,740 --> 00:29:44,640
Some claimed the almost magical ability

522
00:29:44,740 --> 00:29:47,020
to take trash straight from the garbage truck

523
00:29:47,120 --> 00:29:48,740
and extract the recyclable goods.

524
00:29:51,520 --> 00:29:52,380
But many failed.

525
00:29:52,381 --> 00:29:56,820
The problem was most of these organizations

526
00:29:56,920 --> 00:30:00,780
were run by people who were highly motivated

527
00:30:00,880 --> 00:30:04,700
by the environment and didn't know the economics

528
00:30:04,800 --> 00:30:07,000
of scrap recycling.

529
00:30:07,100 --> 00:30:08,960
What we really have to understand

530
00:30:09,060 --> 00:30:12,000
is that this is a business, and it's

531
00:30:12,100 --> 00:30:15,640
got to make money in order to survive.

532
00:30:15,740 --> 00:30:17,400
While private industry faltered,

533
00:30:17,500 --> 00:30:19,600
the government stepped in.

534
00:30:19,601 --> 00:30:23,100
Household recycling began in the mid to late 80s.

535
00:30:23,200 --> 00:30:27,240
Basically, due to legislation and lack of landfill space,

536
00:30:27,340 --> 00:30:29,640
the cities were forced to get into the curbside

537
00:30:29,740 --> 00:30:31,140
or household recycling.

538
00:30:31,240 --> 00:30:34,620
The programs primarily started with a dual stream program,

539
00:30:34,720 --> 00:30:38,180
where the city would put one bin out for newspapers

540
00:30:38,280 --> 00:30:41,260
and one bin out for containers, which would be glass, steel,

541
00:30:41,360 --> 00:30:43,060
plastic, and aluminum.

542
00:30:45,760 --> 00:30:49,600
The dual stream system wasn't an overwhelming success.

543
00:30:49,601 --> 00:30:54,500
Perhaps it was too demanding for a partially committed public.

544
00:30:54,600 --> 00:30:57,240
By the early 1990s, single stream recycling

545
00:30:57,340 --> 00:31:00,840
allowed people to throw everything into one bin.

546
00:31:00,940 --> 00:31:03,140
Single stream was more user friendly,

547
00:31:03,240 --> 00:31:05,940
and the public seemed willing to start recycling.

548
00:31:06,040 --> 00:31:08,160
In many places, the intake of goods doubled.

549
00:31:10,960 --> 00:31:14,800
Today, curbside recycling is more widespread than ever.

550
00:31:14,900 --> 00:31:16,700
The Allen Company is the repository

551
00:31:16,701 --> 00:31:20,700
for recycled materials collected in San Diego, California.

552
00:31:20,800 --> 00:31:23,340
The plant's sorts and bundles used household paper,

553
00:31:23,440 --> 00:31:26,040
cans, and bottles.

554
00:31:26,140 --> 00:31:28,940
We get in about 180 tons of material every day.

555
00:31:29,040 --> 00:31:33,240
As you can see, the trucks dump the material in this big pile.

556
00:31:33,340 --> 00:31:35,560
And this is where the process starts.

557
00:31:35,660 --> 00:31:37,760
The material's put on the conveyor belt,

558
00:31:37,860 --> 00:31:40,600
and it goes up to the top of the pre-start station.

559
00:31:40,700 --> 00:31:44,300
We have people that are picking off the trash and the cardboard.

560
00:31:44,301 --> 00:31:47,100
And then it goes on to the news screen.

561
00:31:47,200 --> 00:31:49,800
The paper surfs over the news screen's wheels,

562
00:31:49,900 --> 00:31:52,600
while the heavier material, like metal cans and plastic

563
00:31:52,700 --> 00:31:55,840
containers, fall between the cracks to a conveyor belt below.

564
00:32:00,980 --> 00:32:03,820
The process relies heavily on manual labor.

565
00:32:03,920 --> 00:32:06,820
As many as 40 hand pickers remove any material that

566
00:32:06,920 --> 00:32:08,320
slips through the separators.

567
00:32:08,420 --> 00:32:10,660
After all the materials are sorted and bailed,

568
00:32:10,760 --> 00:32:12,700
they come out to our yard to be stored,

569
00:32:12,701 --> 00:32:14,900
until we ship them either by rail or container.

570
00:32:18,000 --> 00:32:20,400
Every year, more than 200 billion pounds

571
00:32:20,500 --> 00:32:23,040
of used materials pass through recycling yards

572
00:32:23,140 --> 00:32:26,840
as a first step to becoming new products.

573
00:32:26,940 --> 00:32:30,560
And as more local governments mandate recycling programs,

574
00:32:30,660 --> 00:32:34,160
these yards may soon become the most numerous junkyards

575
00:32:34,260 --> 00:32:35,060
in the country.

576
00:32:35,061 --> 00:32:42,060
Airplanes.

577
00:32:42,061 --> 00:32:46,060
On any given day, nearly 60,000 will be winging their way

578
00:32:46,061 --> 00:32:50,060
across America, including over 30,000 jetliners, some of the

579
00:32:50,061 --> 00:32:52,060
most technologically sophisticated machines

580
00:32:52,061 --> 00:32:56,060
ever created. Millions of individual components work

581
00:32:56,061 --> 00:33:00,060
together to keep these planes aloft.

582
00:33:00,061 --> 00:33:04,060
When they're no longer safe to fly, their last resting plane

583
00:33:04,061 --> 00:33:07,060
Their last resting place is an aviation boneyard.

584
00:33:11,100 --> 00:33:13,900
Today, federal regulations and fear of lawsuits

585
00:33:13,901 --> 00:33:16,480
will keep many yard owners from reselling pieces

586
00:33:16,481 --> 00:33:18,900
off these planes.

587
00:33:18,901 --> 00:33:20,300
But that wasn't always the case.

588
00:33:24,080 --> 00:33:26,420
At one time, used parts were everywhere

589
00:33:26,421 --> 00:33:27,620
in the aircraft industry.

590
00:33:27,621 --> 00:33:32,700
Oroville and Wilbur Wright may have been the very first aviation

591
00:33:32,800 --> 00:33:36,760
salvagers, often using pieces from one prototype aircraft

592
00:33:36,860 --> 00:33:37,700
to build another.

593
00:33:40,840 --> 00:33:44,100
Aviation boneyards grew with the industry.

594
00:33:44,200 --> 00:33:46,980
During World War I, the Curtis Company was one of the first to

595
00:33:47,080 --> 00:33:48,500
mass produce airplanes.

596
00:33:48,600 --> 00:33:53,920
The Jenny, as it was called, became an industry standard,

597
00:33:53,921 --> 00:33:59,600
standard, and eventually, a boneyard regular.

598
00:33:59,700 --> 00:34:01,860
After the war, stunt flyers used these planes

599
00:34:01,960 --> 00:34:04,160
in traveling aerial circuses.

600
00:34:04,260 --> 00:34:08,460
Keeping them in the air meant finding parts.

601
00:34:08,560 --> 00:34:11,840
Most aviation salvage yards have started out

602
00:34:11,940 --> 00:34:13,400
from a need of parts.

603
00:34:13,500 --> 00:34:15,480
Somebody needed parts to build their airplane,

604
00:34:15,580 --> 00:34:18,520
so they bought another one for parts,

605
00:34:18,620 --> 00:34:21,240
and perhaps a second one after that.

606
00:34:21,241 --> 00:34:25,740
First thing you know, you're in the parts business.

607
00:34:25,840 --> 00:34:28,480
World War II saw the greatest buildup of aircraft

608
00:34:28,580 --> 00:34:30,240
in American history.

609
00:34:30,340 --> 00:34:34,220
From 1940 to 1945, nearly 300,000 planes

610
00:34:34,320 --> 00:34:36,860
flew out of homeland factories.

611
00:34:36,960 --> 00:34:38,260
After the war, some of these planes

612
00:34:38,360 --> 00:34:42,220
found work in the civilian world.

613
00:34:42,320 --> 00:34:44,260
Aviation boneyards provided parts

614
00:34:44,360 --> 00:34:46,660
for these converted trainers and crop dusters well

615
00:34:46,760 --> 00:34:50,640
into the 1970s.

616
00:34:50,641 --> 00:34:54,280
But over the years, the aviation salvage business changed.

617
00:34:54,380 --> 00:34:57,940
In the 1980s, many owners found themselves forced out,

618
00:34:58,040 --> 00:35:02,780
not from low scrap or part prices, but from lawsuits.

619
00:35:02,880 --> 00:35:06,180
All the parts we sold, of course, were good parts

620
00:35:06,280 --> 00:35:09,600
and had to be inspected to make sure they were good parts.

621
00:35:09,700 --> 00:35:12,600
But we might have sold a radio, which worked fine.

622
00:35:12,700 --> 00:35:16,840
You put it in an airplane, and a customer might have,

623
00:35:16,940 --> 00:35:20,100
years later, flown into the side of a mountain in bad weather.

624
00:35:20,101 --> 00:35:23,700
We would get named in a lawsuit, and we'd have to defend

625
00:35:23,800 --> 00:35:25,200
ourselves or pay.

626
00:35:25,300 --> 00:35:31,240
We never lost a lawsuit, but we had to hire a lot of lawyers.

627
00:35:31,340 --> 00:35:34,640
And for this reason, a lot of us have gotten back out of the

628
00:35:34,740 --> 00:35:37,760
industry.

629
00:35:37,860 --> 00:35:40,280
Many remaining aviation junkyard owners

630
00:35:40,380 --> 00:35:42,860
looked for other ways to make a living off their flightless

631
00:35:42,960 --> 00:35:44,760
fleets.

632
00:35:44,860 --> 00:35:48,220
One company, the California-based Aviation Warehouse,

633
00:35:48,221 --> 00:35:55,220
two unique business angles, Hollywood and food.

634
00:35:55,320 --> 00:35:57,460
Most people think that this is a salvage yard

635
00:35:57,560 --> 00:36:01,560
for the airline industry, and you can't sell anything

636
00:36:01,660 --> 00:36:04,800
that's not documented to anything that's going to fly.

637
00:36:04,900 --> 00:36:08,900
So this is all just for mock-ups.

638
00:36:09,000 --> 00:36:10,740
Whenever a movie audience thrills

639
00:36:10,840 --> 00:36:13,380
to an exciting plane crash or sees an actor stare

640
00:36:13,480 --> 00:36:17,220
out of a helicopter window, they may be looking at a prop

641
00:36:17,221 --> 00:36:20,220
from this yard.

642
00:36:20,320 --> 00:36:23,060
And if the audience goes out to dinner after the movie,

643
00:36:23,160 --> 00:36:26,220
they may be eating in a prop from this yard.

644
00:36:26,320 --> 00:36:29,460
Over here, I've got a scrapbook of some of the projects

645
00:36:29,560 --> 00:36:34,100
we've done, including some of the airplane restaurants

646
00:36:34,200 --> 00:36:36,700
that we've sent overseas.

647
00:36:36,800 --> 00:36:39,180
This one is in Korea.

648
00:36:39,280 --> 00:36:42,820
In fact, all of these are in Korea.

649
00:36:42,920 --> 00:36:45,340
Movie props and theme restaurants,

650
00:36:45,341 --> 00:36:48,780
a niche industry. But in true junkyard fashion,

651
00:36:48,880 --> 00:36:51,940
this owner has found a way to make money off his secondhand

652
00:36:52,040 --> 00:36:52,740
planes.

653
00:36:55,880 --> 00:36:58,580
Managing the yard is a full-time job,

654
00:36:58,680 --> 00:37:02,100
and keeping track of everything can be a little daunting.

655
00:37:02,200 --> 00:37:03,900
We try to keep it organized.

656
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:05,500
It gets moved around quite a bit.

657
00:37:05,600 --> 00:37:08,000
But yes, we have one section that's for helicopters,

658
00:37:08,100 --> 00:37:10,640
one that's for airliners, another section that's for

659
00:37:10,740 --> 00:37:13,240
small planes, general aviation.

660
00:37:13,241 --> 00:37:15,240
We have a little bit of fighters and that sort.

661
00:37:18,680 --> 00:37:21,240
New acquisitions are always arriving.

662
00:37:21,241 --> 00:37:24,980
Today, three workers easily handle the task of offloading

663
00:37:24,981 --> 00:37:27,880
a Hughes 500 helicopter.

664
00:37:27,881 --> 00:37:31,400
But often the jobs are much bigger.

665
00:37:31,401 --> 00:37:34,200
What if a movie needs a jetliner?

666
00:37:34,201 --> 00:37:39,240
Or worse, half a jetliner?

667
00:37:43,240 --> 00:37:46,480
You have to cut it up small enough to get it on a truck

668
00:37:46,580 --> 00:37:47,480
when it leaves here.

669
00:37:47,580 --> 00:37:49,680
Basically, what you'll use is like a chainsaw,

670
00:37:49,780 --> 00:37:53,580
which has a carbide blade on it, and you'll cut the plane up.

671
00:37:59,480 --> 00:38:02,000
The only other option is to have the plane leave the yard

672
00:38:02,100 --> 00:38:02,900
in one piece.

673
00:38:03,000 --> 00:38:07,640
For aviation salvagers, this can be a heart and traffic

674
00:38:07,740 --> 00:38:08,840
stopping experience.

675
00:38:08,841 --> 00:38:14,180
experience. Though great in the air, 747s

676
00:38:14,181 --> 00:38:20,380
don't do well on road trips. Still, this junkyard's ultimate

677
00:38:20,381 --> 00:38:24,680
service may be to the planes themselves.

678
00:38:24,681 --> 00:38:27,800
Aviation Warehouse boasts one of the largest collections of

679
00:38:27,801 --> 00:38:31,000
aircraft technical manuals in existence.

680
00:38:31,001 --> 00:38:33,740
Mechanics and airplane buffs alike regularly search the

681
00:38:33,741 --> 00:38:38,840
library.

682
00:38:38,841 --> 00:38:42,140
This junkyard owner hopes to keep these old-time flying

683
00:38:42,240 --> 00:38:45,480
treasures in the sky, if not through spare parts,

684
00:38:45,580 --> 00:38:47,480
then by providing knowledge instead.

685
00:38:54,080 --> 00:38:56,900
In the junkyard, nothing goes to waste.

686
00:38:59,100 --> 00:39:02,900
On the outskirts of Madison, Wisconsin, one old-time scrapper

687
00:39:03,000 --> 00:39:05,500
has found a unique way of sticking to that creed.

688
00:39:08,840 --> 00:39:15,840
Tom Every has been in the scrap business his entire life.

689
00:39:15,841 --> 00:39:17,840
TOM EVERY HAS BEEN IN THE SCRAP BUSINESS HIS ENTIRE LIFE.

690
00:39:17,841 --> 00:39:23,840
I think that we all evolve, and I recycled about every kind

691
00:39:23,841 --> 00:39:24,840
of thing there is.

692
00:39:24,841 --> 00:39:27,840
And then when I got all done, I realized, my gosh, there's

693
00:39:27,841 --> 00:39:29,840
nothing around even to look at.

694
00:39:29,841 --> 00:39:34,840
And so I decided in this balanced part of my life I would

695
00:39:34,841 --> 00:39:36,840
build up instead of tear down.

696
00:39:36,841 --> 00:39:37,840
I guess.

697
00:39:37,841 --> 00:39:43,840
Building up has been Tom's passion for nearly 20 years.

698
00:39:43,841 --> 00:39:46,840
That's when he first stopped scrapping and assumed the mantle

699
00:39:46,841 --> 00:39:51,840
of his creative alter ego, a man he calls Dr. Evermore.

700
00:39:51,841 --> 00:39:55,840
The doctor's years of hard work and artistic vision have paid

701
00:39:55,841 --> 00:40:00,840
off in a creation dubbed the Forevertron.

702
00:40:00,841 --> 00:40:07,840
Forevertron. Built as an ode to immortality,

703
00:40:07,841 --> 00:40:12,080
the Forevertron's purpose is to launch the good doctor back into

704
00:40:12,081 --> 00:40:15,680
the heavens on a magnetic lightning force beam.

705
00:40:15,681 --> 00:40:18,800
And to do that takes a lot of stuff.

706
00:40:21,800 --> 00:40:26,800
The Forevertron is about 130 foot long and about 65 feet

707
00:40:26,801 --> 00:40:31,400
to 5 feet high and up to 120 foot wide.

708
00:40:31,401 --> 00:40:35,700
It weighs a little over 400 ton.

709
00:40:35,701 --> 00:40:39,840
As you may have guessed, the machine doesn't really work.

710
00:40:39,841 --> 00:40:42,240
But at one time, almost everything on it did.

711
00:40:45,260 --> 00:40:47,320
The Forevertron is a creative combination

712
00:40:47,321 --> 00:40:50,460
of artistic vision and gross tonnage,

713
00:40:50,461 --> 00:40:53,320
a metallic fantasy where art and history collide

714
00:40:53,321 --> 00:40:55,920
in some unexpected ways.

715
00:40:55,921 --> 00:40:58,960
Incorporated into the front of the artwork is part of the real

716
00:40:58,961 --> 00:41:02,400
life decontamination chamber used by the Apollo astronauts

717
00:41:02,401 --> 00:41:06,000
after returning from the moon. In contrast, one of the oldest

718
00:41:06,001 --> 00:41:08,800
pieces is anything but space age.

719
00:41:08,801 --> 00:41:12,160
It's a 19th century bipolar dynamo from a very famous

720
00:41:12,161 --> 00:41:15,920
inventor. Well, Big War was built by

721
00:41:15,921 --> 00:41:21,200
Thomas Edison. It's the number four one that

722
00:41:21,201 --> 00:41:22,200
ever built.

723
00:41:26,200 --> 00:41:29,200
Because the doctor is always finding new things,

724
00:41:29,300 --> 00:41:32,140
the Forevertron will never be finished.

725
00:41:32,240 --> 00:41:37,580
Like the piles in a scrapyard, it will simply continue to grow.

726
00:41:37,680 --> 00:41:41,420
I would say big copper brew kettles and candy pots

727
00:41:41,520 --> 00:41:44,220
and anything that had to do with copper and brass.

728
00:41:44,320 --> 00:41:45,960
I've got mountains of it.

729
00:41:46,060 --> 00:41:48,600
I've got at least a couple thousand ton of it

730
00:41:48,700 --> 00:41:49,700
laying around here.

731
00:41:49,701 --> 00:41:51,900
I'll never live long enough to use it all up,

732
00:41:52,000 --> 00:41:54,340
but I just love to see if there's anything that's

733
00:41:54,440 --> 00:41:57,340
got some possibilities.

734
00:41:57,440 --> 00:42:02,140
And I thoroughly enjoy finding things and then putting them

735
00:42:02,240 --> 00:42:03,340
into something else again.

736
00:42:06,160 --> 00:42:09,460
With the heart of a scrapper and the eye of an artist,

737
00:42:09,560 --> 00:42:12,960
Dr. Evermore's vision isn't too far removed from that of every

738
00:42:13,060 --> 00:42:14,260
other junkyard worker.

739
00:42:19,700 --> 00:42:26,700
Well, maybe it's a little removed.

740
00:42:26,800 --> 00:42:30,840
But the truth is, this is an industry where one person's junk

741
00:42:30,940 --> 00:42:36,920
is another's masterpiece, and where value and beauty are truly

742
00:42:37,020 --> 00:42:40,820
in the eye of the beholder.

